# Rank 1810 / CVE-2024-53947 (recorded CWE-89; the axis is CWE-184, an
# incomplete list of disallowed values): apache/superset executes SQL Lab and
# chart SQL behind a function-name denylist. BaseEngineSpec.execute
# (superset/db_engine_specs/base.py:1825) reads
# current_app.config["DISALLOWED_SQL_FUNCTIONS"], asks
# sql_parse.check_sql_functions_exist(query, disallowed, engine) whether the
# statement calls any named function, raises DisallowedSQLFunction when it
# does, and otherwise runs cursor.execute(query). The denylist
# (superset/config.py:1278) named four postgresql functions -- version,
# query_to_xml, inet_server_addr, inet_client_addr -- and omitted the rest of
# PostgreSQL's documented XML-producing family: query_to_xml_and_xmlschema,
# table_to_xml, table_to_xml_and_xmlschema and their siblings all take a
# query/table/database (or serialize one opened) and return it as XML, so
# query_to_xml_and_xmlschema('select ...') executes the attacker's nested
# query text through a spelling the screen never consults. The fixed revision
# 0e002826 adds four strings to the set literal (the advisory names
# query_to_xml_and_xmlschema, table_to_xml and table_to_xml_and_xmlschema;
# the commit adds database_to_xml as well) and touches no code path. This is
# the second pass over the same list (CVE-2024-39887 added the first four),
# which is the shape's own character: the project enumerates the family one
# advisory at a time.
#
# Measured on both revisions, three --cache off runs apiece over
# superset/config.py + superset/db_engine_specs + superset/sql_parse.py:
# 30 findings and 37 review flags on the vulnerable parent and the same 30
# and 37 on the fixed revision, deterministic within a revision, and the
# only differences between the two reports are the config.py
# hardcoded-secret rows shifting nine lines down because the fix adds nine
# lines to the file. The full findings view was read both ways -- the
# default report, `-flags with` and `-flags only` -- and none of the 37
# flags touches the denylist or the consult. The consumer's
# cursor.execute(query) is labelled code.SqlExecution at base.py:1831
# (python.sinks, syntactic) and VYQL-INJ-203 fires on sixteen other engine
# specs' dynamic SQL in this tree, but not on base.py, because on the real
# tree the request payload -> Query model -> execute_sql_statements ->
# spec.execute chain severs taint at the ORM persistence boundary, and that
# path is identical in both revisions either way -- running user SQL is SQL
# Lab's purpose, and the guard call is present in both. The denylist has
# exactly one non-test consumer (grep over the tree), and the 1380
# counterweight test fails: the fixed revision touches no guarded function,
# only the config set's contents.
#
# Reuse is checked before minting. The ontology's denylist-gap vocabulary
# (code.IncompleteDangerousUploadExtensionBlocklist,
# code.SandboxHostObjectMemberDenylistGap, code.IncompleteIpDenylist,
# code.H2oJdbcDisallowedParameterDenylistGap,
# code.IncompleteShellSubstitutionDenylist,
# code.IncompleteKeyDenylistGuardedReadPrototypePollution) is the right class
# of judgement but each concept's vulnerableTo threat and reviewCondition
# name a different surface (upload extensions, sandbox members, IP ranges,
# JDBC connection parameters, shell substitutions, prototype-pollution
# keys), so reuse would mis-file this finding. The siblings minted since the
# first pass are the same pattern one surface at a time, none of them SQL.
#
# Minting a SQL-function-denylist sibling does not discriminate, and the
# reason is set arithmetic rather than judgement: PostgreSQL documents
# fifteen members of the XML-producing family (query_, cursor_, table_,
# database_ and schema_ each as _to_xml, _to_xmlschema and
# _to_xml_and_xmlschema); the vulnerable set names one of them and the
# patched set names five, so a documentation-faithful completeness predicate
# is false on both revisions -- ten documented spellings, among them the
# whole cursor_ and schema_ branches, are still absent from the patched set.
# The only list that separates the two trees is the fixing commit's own
# four strings. That is the 1394 trap in data form, and dropping the
# patched-form reject from the spec to ship the documentation-derived
# version would report the fixed revision, which is a false positive against
# the fix on the very tree this corpus pins. The corpus sweep is also
# empty: across the clones under /tmp/cve-work no other file names any
# XML-family member or declares any SQL-function denylist (cross-checked
# pg_read_file/pg_sleep/dblink/lo_import; the only hits are the ace-builds
# pgsql syntax-highlighting mode files and two lexer/test fixtures), so the
# binding would fire on one product's one config file.
#
# Held, the configuration-content family (1514, 1535, 1540, 1556, 1557,
# 1593; missing-entry vocabulary like 1609/1614), not an engine gap: no
# construct is mishandled -- config.py parses, the set literal is simply not
# vocabulary, and the distinguishing information is four strings in it.
#
# All cases assert reject VYQL-INJ-001 (the taint rule consuming
# code.SqlExecution): nothing fires today because the query arrives as a
# parameter behind the ORM boundary and the denylist's contents carry no
# label. When SQL-function-denylist vocabulary lands, the first case must
# flip to expect while the patched, documentation-complete and
# no-consult-override cases stay reject -- and landing it requires the
# vocabulary to state which family members a screen must name, not which
# ones one advisory happened to list.

test "rank1810: pg function denylist consult runs user SQL through a screen that names one family member"
  lang python
  reject VYQL-INJ-001
  file superset/config.py
  code
  ```
  DISALLOWED_SQL_FUNCTIONS: dict[str, set[str]] = {
      "postgresql": {"version", "query_to_xml", "inet_server_addr", "inet_client_addr"},
      "clickhouse": {"url"},
      "mysql": {"version"},
  }
  ```
  file superset/db_engine_specs/base.py
  code
  ```
  from flask import current_app

  from superset import sql_parse


  class DisallowedSQLFunction(Exception):
      def __init__(self, functions):
          super().__init__(f"disallowed SQL functions: {functions}")


  class BaseEngineSpec:
      engine = "postgresql"

      @classmethod
      def execute(cls, cursor, query, database, **kwargs):
          disallowed_functions = current_app.config["DISALLOWED_SQL_FUNCTIONS"].get(
              cls.engine, set()
          )
          if sql_parse.check_sql_functions_exist(query, disallowed_functions, cls.engine):
              raise DisallowedSQLFunction(disallowed_functions)

          cursor.execute(query)
  ```

